To that end she makes the perfect study for me as a Jr to look at as an example and say "OK this is a person that has value". To really understand that you need to see her work check this talk out: [placeURL], it was one of her early wins and really illustrates how bad malware authors can actually be when it comes to obfuscation (or lack thereof as the case may be), when you consider that this was on the play store for some time it does bring in to question google and their review process. Because if there where more complex attacks have taken place. this still caught allot of people off guard and is still an issue today. 

Google has a program called "bouncer" and is and interesting bit of software that aims to find anything deemed to be malicious: but as many people have proven over the years: it is very easy to trick and get your code to play nice with when in it, then be really mean when on a real phone. Due to the work she did: this kind of proves that it is not as secure as their marketing team say that it is. Though if you really want to see how bad that can get check out John Overhide's work on it where he proved that simple tricks that all basic security people knew about in the 00's can get horrific things to happen. One example of this is that it can hide dll's (on older android versions) because of the fact that there was no code signing used in this process  and that makes getting code to act badly very easy if google can't see what it is doing and then it just grabs down a URL(to load the dll), then there is not much they can do to stop this with no real verification of the code. This as a combo with the fact that bouncer can be tricked into loading bad things(mostly because malware can tell it's in bouncer(again go read Johns work)), it makes for a nasty attack path for bad actors.


Really it feels cheap to blame some of these systems and benchmark them on such things in a way because they are not motivated by security: but more profit and functionality: which any security person worth their salt; knows kills security. It is an age old problem that we keep having in security that defines the internal battle between people in tech, we want people to make cool things and so it's NOT cool of us to simply finger wag and call people "idoits"(be that true or not): because all bit's aside (pun intended). One of the best reasons for this other than it is simply not nice to disagree by insulting someone: is that we depend on this side of things in order to function. There is no securing the technology if it does not exit? 

